title: Codefinger / S3 Ransomware
page: breaches/codefinger-s3.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: With compromised or publicly exposed AWS keys and no AWS vulnerability, Codefinger encrypted S3 buckets under a key AWS never stored and set the data to delete within seven days.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: SSE-C | abused as the encryption primitive | AWS's own encryption with customer-provided keys
stat: 7 days | until S3 Lifecycle deletes the data | policies applied as ransom pressure
stat: AES | attacker-held AES-256 key | no server-side copy
The threat actor, Codefinger, is financially motivated and demanded a Bitcoin ransom. Halcyon, which disclosed the campaign on January 13, 2025, observed at least two victim organisations.
notes:
Start with three of the figures the page leads with. Its fourth names the threat actor, and sits below them.

SSE-C is AWS's own Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys, and Codefinger abused it as the encryption primitive. Seven days is the deadline: Codefinger applied S3 Lifecycle policies to delete the encrypted objects within seven days, as ransom pressure. AES-256 is the attacker-held key. The attacker generates and holds it locally, and AWS stores only an HMAC of it, so there is no server-side copy.

Codefinger is the threat actor, financially motivated, and it demanded payment in Bitcoin.

One more fact frames the rest of the deck: Halcyon, which disclosed the campaign on January 13, 2025, observed at least two victim organisations. Keep the words at least, and keep the attribution to Halcyon.

S3 is Amazon Simple Storage Service, AWS's object storage.

== What happened
layout: text
Codefinger, a financially motivated actor, used compromised or publicly exposed AWS access keys to encrypt victims' S3 buckets with SSE-C. It applied S3 Lifecycle policies to delete the encrypted objects within seven days and demanded payment in Bitcoin.
notes:
Here is the page's summary.

A financially motivated actor tracked as Codefinger used compromised or publicly exposed AWS access keys to encrypt victims' S3 buckets, using AWS's own Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys, SSE-C. Because the attacker generates and holds the AES-256 key locally, and AWS stores only an HMAC of that key, victims cannot decrypt their data without paying.

Codefinger applied S3 Lifecycle policies to delete the encrypted objects within seven days as ransom pressure, and demanded payment in Bitcoin.

No AWS vulnerability was exploited. The page says the entire campaign relied on abusing valid credentials and native AWS features.

Halcyon disclosed the campaign on January 13, 2025, and observed at least two victim organisations. Apart from that date and January 2025 in its header, the page's only dates are for AWS's later change to the SSE-C default, which comes up with the defenses. So this deck has no timeline of the incident.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Credential Acquisition | Exposed AWS keys | T1552
step: 02 | Initial Access | Valid AWS credentials | T1078.004
step: 03 | Discovery | Not described in the sources | T1619
step: 04 | Impact | SSE-C encryption | T1486
step: 05 | Extortion | 7-day delete, Bitcoin ransom | T1657
Arrows follow the page's stage order.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's five stages, each with its phase and ATT&CK technique. ATT&CK is MITRE's knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques.

Stage 01, Credential Acquisition: Codefinger obtained AWS access keys from public exposure or prior compromise. Stage 02, Initial Access: it authenticated to the victim's AWS account with them. Stage 03, Discovery: the cited sources do not say how Codefinger found the buckets and objects it encrypted, and the page keeps the stage to mark that gap. Stage 04, Impact: objects were overwritten with copies encrypted under an attacker-held AES-256 SSE-C key. Stage 05, Extortion: an S3 Lifecycle policy to delete the encrypted objects within seven days, and a ransom note demanding Bitcoin.

The next slides take each stage in turn.

== AWS keys from public exposure or prior compromise
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Credential Acquisition
technique: T1552 | Unsecured Credentials
fact: Credential | a long-lived AWS IAM access key ID and secret access key
fact: Common leak paths | Git commits, public buckets, CI/CD logs, hardcoded app config
fact: Why it worked | never rotated, no expiry, no detection of use from new sources
Halcyon assessed the actor used keys that could read and write S3 objects, so no privilege escalation or AWS vulnerability was needed.
notes:
Stage 01 is where the keys came from. The page maps it to T1552, Unsecured Credentials.

Codefinger sourced valid AWS access key IDs and secret access keys that had been publicly disclosed or otherwise compromised. The page does not say where any victim's keys leaked from.

The leak paths on the slide are the page's common ones. Long-lived IAM access keys routinely leak through committed source code, exposed config files, CI logs and public buckets, and scanners harvest them within minutes of exposure. IAM is identity and access management, and CI/CD is continuous integration and continuous delivery.

Halcyon assessed that the actor used keys with permissions to read and write S3 objects, so no privilege escalation or AWS vulnerability was needed. Keep that attribution: it is Halcyon's assessment. Possession of a working key pair was sufficient.

The next slide explains AWS access keys in general terms.

== What an AWS access key is
layout: background
source: AWS IAM User Guide, manage access keys for IAM users | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_access-keys.html
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary, Ephemeral Credentials (csoh.org)
Access keys are long-term credentials for an IAM user: an access key ID and a secret access key, used together to sign requests to the AWS CLI or API. Ephemeral credentials, by contrast, last minutes to hours.
notes:
Background, from the AWS IAM User Guide and the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

AWS describes access keys as long-term credentials for an IAM user. You use them to sign programmatic requests to the AWS command line interface or the AWS API, directly or through an AWS SDK. Each key has two parts, an access key ID and a secret access key, and both are used together to authenticate a request.

AWS calls IAM users with access keys an account security risk, and warns that giving your access keys to unauthorized parties might give them permanent access to your account.

The csoh.org glossary describes ephemeral credentials as short-lived, minutes to hours, issued by STS or OIDC federation, and calls them the replacement for long-lived access keys. STS is AWS Security Token Service, and OIDC is OpenID Connect.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-ephemeral-credentials

== The stolen keys passed authentication with no anomaly
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Permissions | s3:GetObject and s3:PutObject on target buckets
fact: Enough to | read existing objects and overwrite them with encrypted copies
fact: CloudTrail | object-level calls recorded only with S3 data events on
No cited source says whether the victims had S3 data events on. Either way, no alert fires by default on legitimate-looking S3 calls from a new IP or region.
notes:
Stage 02 is initial access. The page maps it to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts.

Using the compromised keys, Codefinger authenticated to the victim's AWS account as a legitimate principal, through the AWS SDK, the CLI or the API. Because the request came from a valid IAM identity, it passed authentication with no anomaly.

The keys carried s3:GetObject and s3:PutObject permissions, which were enough to read existing objects and overwrite them with encrypted copies. Those two permissions come back in the defenses.

AWS CloudTrail is the AWS audit log. By default, the page says, it records bucket-level S3 calls such as lifecycle changes, but it records object-level calls such as PutObject and CopyObject only when S3 data events are turned on, which costs extra. No cited source says whether the victims had them on. Either way, no alert fires by default on legitimate-looking S3 calls from a new IP or region.

== The sources do not say how Codefinger found the buckets
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Discovery
technique: T1619 | Cloud Storage Object Discovery
fact: Reported | no listing step in any cited source
fact: Calls, if used | ListBuckets (s3:ListAllMyBuckets), ListObjectsV2 (s3:ListBucket)
fact: Typical targets | backups, application data, customer records, media assets
Listing buckets and objects takes ordinary read calls that generate no exploitation signal, but no source reports that Codefinger made them.
notes:
Stage 03 is a gap in the public record, and the page keeps it to mark that gap. The page still labels it Discovery and tags it T1619, Cloud Storage Object Discovery.

The cited sources do not say how Codefinger found the buckets and objects it encrypted. Halcyon's account goes from locating keys with s3:GetObject and s3:PutObject permissions straight to encrypting files, and Arctic Wolf, The Register and AWS's guidance describe no listing step either.

Listing buckets and objects takes ordinary read calls that generate no exploitation signal, but no source reports that Codefinger made them. If listing was used, the calls would be ListBuckets, which needs the s3:ListAllMyBuckets permission, and ListObjectsV2, which needs s3:ListBucket. Do not present either as something Codefinger did.

The page lists backups, application data, customer records and media assets as typical targets. It does not say what data any victim held.

No defense on the page is tied to this stage. That gap is one of the discussion questions at the end.

== Objects overwritten with copies encrypted under an attacker-held key
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Impact
technique: T1486 | Data Encrypted for Impact
fact: Method | each target object re-written with SSE-C, key in the headers
fact: Operation | CopyObject, per AWS, which does not name the actor
fact: Key custody | AES-256, generated locally by attacker; AWS keeps only an HMAC
fact: Result | data unrecoverable without the attacker's key, even by AWS
A native encryption feature became a ransomware primitive; no malware was deployed.
notes:
Stage 04 is the impact. The page maps it to T1486, Data Encrypted for Impact.

Codefinger encrypted each target object by re-writing it with Server-Side Encryption using Customer-Provided Keys, SSE-C. The attacker generated an AES-256 key locally and supplied it in the request's x-amz-server-side-encryption-customer-algorithm and x-amz-server-side-encryption-customer-key headers. AES-256 is the Advanced Encryption Standard with a 256-bit key.

Halcyon does not name the S3 operation. AWS, in guidance Halcyon says was published in response to its report, describes a large number of S3 CopyObject operations using SSE-C overwriting objects and re-encrypting them with a new key, without naming the actor. Keep that caveat. A copy needs s3:GetObject on the source and s3:PutObject on the destination, the two permissions Halcyon names.

AWS performs the encryption but does not store the key. CloudTrail logs only an HMAC of it, which is insufficient to reconstruct the key. Once complete, the data is unrecoverable without the attacker's key, even by AWS.

The page's point is that this turns a native, legitimate encryption feature into a ransomware primitive with no malware deployed.

== Who holds the key after an SSE-C overwrite
layout: table
row: Party | Holds | What follows
row: Codefinger | the AES-256 key, generated and stored locally | demands Bitcoin for it as the decryption key
row: AWS | only an HMAC of the key | cannot decrypt the objects
row: Victim | objects encrypted with a key it does not control | unrecoverable without the attacker's key
CloudTrail logs only an HMAC of the key, which is insufficient to reconstruct it.
notes:
This slide draws the key custody the page describes at stage 04.

The attacker generated the AES-256 key locally and kept it. AWS performed the encryption but did not store the key. The page says AWS keeps only an HMAC of it, and that CloudTrail logs only an HMAC of it, which is insufficient to reconstruct the key. So AWS cannot decrypt the objects. HMAC stands for hash-based message authentication code.

The victim is left with objects encrypted under a key it does not control, unrecoverable without the attacker's key. That key is what the ransom demand asks Bitcoin for.

The page does not say whether any victim paid.

The next slide explains SSE-C in general terms, beside the other S3 encryption options.

== What SSE-C is, beside SSE-S3 and SSE-KMS
layout: background
source: Amazon S3 User Guide, specifying SSE-C | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/specifying-s3-c-encryption.html
credit: Also from Protecting data with server-side encryption (docs.aws.amazon.com)
With SSE-C, you supply the key with each request. S3 encrypts, removes the key from memory and stores a salted HMAC instead, which cannot be used to derive the key. Lose the key, AWS says, and you lose the object.
notes:
Background, from the Amazon S3 User Guide, in general terms.

S3 offers four mutually exclusive server-side encryption options, set apart by how you manage the keys and how many layers of encryption apply. SSE-S3, with Amazon S3 managed keys, is the default encryption configuration for every bucket. SSE-KMS uses keys in AWS Key Management Service, which gives you more control. DSSE-KMS applies two layers of encryption. With SSE-C, you manage the encryption keys, and S3 manages the encryption as it writes to disks and the decryption when you access your objects.

With SSE-C, request headers name the algorithm, AES256, and carry the key itself. S3 applies AES-256 encryption, then removes the key from memory, and to read the object you must provide the same key again. Instead of the key, S3 stores a randomly salted HMAC of it to validate future requests, and that value cannot be used to derive the key or to decrypt the object. AWS draws the consequence: if you lose the encryption key, you lose the object.

Both AWS pages now also note a later change: in April 2026, S3 began disabling SSE-C by default for new general purpose buckets. That came after this campaign, and the defense slides give the page's account of it.

Also from Protecting data with server-side encryption: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/serv-side-encryption.html

== A seven-day lifecycle deletion and a Bitcoin ransom note
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Extortion
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Pressure | S3 Lifecycle policy, permanent object deletion within 7 days
fact: Ransom note | in each affected directory, with a Bitcoin address
fact: Warning | not to alter account permissions or attempt recovery
fact: Victims | at least two organisations hit, as Halcyon reported
AWS never held the key, so victims who did not pay in time lost the data outright.
notes:
Stage 05 is the extortion. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft.

To force fast payment, Codefinger applied an S3 Lifecycle policy configured to permanently delete the encrypted objects within seven days. The attacker left a ransom note in each affected directory with a Bitcoin address and payment instructions, and warned victims not to alter account permissions or attempt recovery.

Because AWS never held the SSE-C key, victims who did not pay before the lifecycle timer expired lost the data outright. The page does not say whether any victim paid.

Halcyon reported that at least two organisations were hit. Keep the words at least, and keep the attribution to Halcyon. The page names no victim.

The next slide explains S3 Lifecycle in general terms.

== What an S3 Lifecycle configuration does
layout: background
source: Amazon S3 User Guide, managing the lifecycle of objects | https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html
An S3 Lifecycle configuration is a set of rules S3 applies to a group of objects. Transition actions move objects to another storage class. Expiration actions define when objects expire, and S3 deletes expired objects on your behalf.
notes:
Background, from the Amazon S3 User Guide, in general terms.

S3 Lifecycle helps you store objects cost effectively, by moving them to lower-cost storage classes or by deleting expired objects on your behalf. You manage it with an S3 Lifecycle configuration on the bucket: a set of rules that define actions Amazon S3 applies to a group of objects.

There are two kinds of action. Transition actions define when objects move to another storage class. Expiration actions define when objects expire, and Amazon S3 deletes the expired objects on your behalf.

AWS adds that when you add a lifecycle configuration to a bucket, its rules apply to existing objects as well as to objects added later.

== No AWS vulnerability, no malware: valid keys and native features
layout: compare
col: What Codefinger brought | Valid AWS keys, publicly exposed or otherwise compromised | An AES-256 key generated locally | A ransom note with a Bitcoin address
col: Native AWS features it abused | SSE-C, AWS's own encryption with customer-provided keys | An S3 Lifecycle policy to delete within seven days
No AWS vulnerability was exploited. The entire campaign relied on abusing valid credentials and native AWS features.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws.

No AWS vulnerability was exploited, and at stage 04 the page says the encryption ran with no malware deployed. The page's summary puts the pattern in one line: the entire campaign relied on abusing valid credentials and native AWS features.

On its own side, Codefinger brought valid AWS keys, publicly exposed or otherwise compromised, an AES-256 key it generated locally, and a ransom note with a Bitcoin address.

From AWS, it used SSE-C to encrypt the objects, and an S3 Lifecycle policy to delete the encrypted objects within seven days. How it found the objects is the gap at stage 03.

== Five defenses against five stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05
row: Restrict or disable SSE-C | 04
row: Eliminate long-lived keys, rotate the rest | 01
row: Least privilege on S3 permissions | 02
row: Versioning, Object Lock, isolated backups | 04 | 05
row: CloudTrail data events and alerts | 02 | 04 | 05
No defense on the page is tied to stage 03, the step the sources do not describe.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's five defenses against the five stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something that stage has.

Restricting SSE-C answers stage 04: it denies s3:PutObject requests carrying the SSE-C algorithm header, and the page says it closes the exact primitive Codefinger abused. Eliminating long-lived keys and rotating the rest answers stage 01, where keys were never rotated and had no expiry. Least privilege answers stage 02: it names the s3:GetObject and s3:PutObject permissions the keys carried. Versioning answers stage 04, because an SSE-C overwrite then does not destroy the original, and Object Lock answers stage 05, defeating the seven-day lifecycle pressure. The CloudTrail alerts answer stage 04 through PutObject and CopyObject events carrying the SSE-C algorithm header, stage 05 through PutBucketLifecycle events, and stage 02 through the S3 data events it says are off by default and through unfamiliar source IPs on existing keys.

No defense is tied to stage 03, the step the sources do not describe. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Restrict or disable unneeded SSE-C
layout: text
- Most workloads use SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS, not SSE-C.
- Deny s3:PutObject in a bucket policy or SCP when a request carries the SSE-C algorithm header.
- An attacker then cannot encrypt your objects with a key you do not control.
notes:
This defense answers stage 04. The page says it closes the exact primitive Codefinger abused.

Most workloads use SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS, not SSE-C. Where you do not need SSE-C, add an S3 bucket policy or an SCP that denies s3:PutObject when the request carries the x-amz-server-side-encryption-customer-algorithm header. Then an attacker cannot encrypt your objects with a key you do not control.

An SCP is a service control policy, an AWS Organizations guardrail. KMS is AWS Key Management Service.

The page adds that AWS has since changed the default, which the next slide covers.

== AWS has since changed the SSE-C default
layout: compare
col: SSE-C now off by default | New general purpose buckets, with two exceptions in AWS's FAQ | Existing buckets in accounts that held no SSE-C encrypted objects | Blocked SSE-C writes get a 403 AccessDenied error
col: Check which case is yours | Accounts that already had SSE-C objects: AWS changed no bucket's configuration | Turning SSE-C back on takes PutBucketEncryption and s3:PutEncryptionConfiguration
AWS announced the change on November 19, 2025 and deployed it from April 6, 2026, completing it that month, per AWS.
notes:
This is the second half of the first defense, as the page gives it. AWS has since changed the default.

In a change announced on November 19, 2025 and deployed from April 6, 2026, completed that month per AWS, S3 disables SSE-C by default on new general purpose buckets. AWS also disabled it on existing buckets in accounts that held no SSE-C encrypted objects. AWS's FAQ lists Middle East (Bahrain) and Middle East (UAE) as exceptions for new buckets.

Writes that specify SSE-C to a bucket where it is blocked are rejected with a 403 AccessDenied error. In accounts that already had SSE-C objects, AWS changed no bucket's configuration, so check which case yours is. Turning SSE-C back on for a bucket takes a PutBucketEncryption call and the s3:PutEncryptionConfiguration permission.

The change came after this campaign, which Halcyon disclosed on January 13, 2025. The page does not say it was a response to Codefinger.

== Fewer long-lived keys, least privilege on S3
layout: compare
col: Long-lived keys (stage 01) | Prefer short-lived credentials via IAM roles, SSO and STS | Rotate unavoidable keys regularly, and scope them tightly | Scan repos, CI logs and buckets for exposed keys
col: Least privilege (stage 02) | Grant s3:PutObject and s3:GetObject only to principals and buckets that require them | A leaked key with narrow, read-only scope cannot re-encrypt your data | Audit for over-permissioned keys and wildcard S3 policies
The page says nearly every cloud ransomware case starts with a leaked long-lived IAM key.
notes:
Two defenses work on the keys themselves.

The first answers stage 01. Eliminate long-lived IAM access keys and rotate what remains. Prefer short-lived credentials through IAM roles, SSO and STS. Where keys are unavoidable, rotate them regularly, scope them tightly, and scan your repos, CI logs and buckets for exposed keys. The page says nearly every cloud ransomware case starts with a leaked long-lived IAM key; keep the word nearly. SSO is single sign-on, and STS is AWS Security Token Service, which issues short-lived credentials.

The second answers stage 02. Apply least privilege to S3 permissions: grant s3:PutObject and s3:GetObject only to the principals and buckets that require them. A leaked key with narrow, read-only scope cannot re-encrypt your data. Audit for over-permissioned keys and wildcard S3 policies.

== Keep the originals: Versioning, Object Lock, isolated backups
layout: text
- Versioning keeps prior versions, so an SSE-C overwrite does not destroy the original.
- Object Lock (compliance mode) blocks deletion within a retention window, defeating the 7-day lifecycle pressure.
- Keep backups in a separate account the leaked key cannot reach.
notes:
This defense answers stages 04 and 05.

Enable S3 Versioning and Object Lock, and keep isolated backups. Versioning preserves prior object versions, so an SSE-C overwrite does not destroy the original. That answers stage 04.

Object Lock in compliance mode blocks deletion within a retention window, which the page says defeats the seven-day lifecycle pressure of stage 05.

Finally, maintain backups in a separate account that the leaked key cannot reach.

== Alert on SSE-C use and lifecycle changes
layout: table
row: Monitor CloudTrail for | Stage
row: PutObject and CopyObject events carrying the SSE-C algorithm header | 04, SSE-C encryption
row: PutBucketLifecycle events | 05, the deletion policy
row: Unfamiliar source IPs on existing keys | 02, no default alert on a new IP
First turn on S3 data events, which are off by default and cost extra. Then route these to your SIEM.
notes:
The last defense is detection, and it touches three stages.

CloudTrail records S3 object-level calls only as data events, which are off by default and cost extra, so turn them on for the buckets you need to watch. That is the logging gap stage 02 describes.

Then monitor for PutObject and CopyObject events whose request parameters carry x-amz-server-side-encryption-customer-algorithm, the header stage 04's requests carried. Monitor for PutBucketLifecycle events, the CloudTrail name for PutBucketLifecycleConfiguration, which the page pairs with new deletion policies like the one at stage 05. And monitor for unfamiliar source IPs on existing keys, the gap stage 02 describes, where valid-credential access from a new IP raised no default alert.

Route these to your SIEM so unusual S3 encryption or new deletion policies trigger an immediate response. SIEM is security information and event management: central log collection, correlation and alerting.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Which of your accounts kept SSE-C when AWS changed the default?
check: Where SSE-C is unneeded, do bucket policies or SCPs deny PutObject with its header?
check: Could IAM roles, SSO or STS replace your remaining long-lived access keys?
check: Are remaining keys rotated and tightly scoped, and do you scan for exposed ones?
check: Is s3:PutObject granted only to the principals and buckets that need it?
check: Do your buckets use Versioning and Object Lock, with backups in a separate account?
check: With S3 data events on, would SSE-C writes, lifecycle changes or unfamiliar IPs alert?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask which of your accounts already had SSE-C objects when AWS changed the default, because AWS changed no bucket's configuration in those accounts. Then ask whether a bucket policy or SCP denies s3:PutObject requests that carry the SSE-C algorithm header, wherever you do not need SSE-C.

Ask which long-lived IAM access keys remain, and whether IAM roles, SSO or STS could replace them. For the keys that must stay, ask whether they are rotated regularly and scoped tightly, and whether anything scans repos, CI logs and buckets for exposed keys.

Ask whether s3:PutObject and s3:GetObject are granted only to the principals and buckets that require them.

Ask whether buckets use Versioning and Object Lock in compliance mode, with backups in a separate account a leaked key cannot reach.

Finally, ask whether S3 data events are on for the buckets you watch, and whether alerts on SSE-C headers, PutBucketLifecycle events and unfamiliar source IPs reach your SIEM.

== Where would an SSE-C write still succeed?
layout: question
Codefinger re-wrote objects with SSE-C under a key it held. AWS has since disabled SSE-C by default on new general purpose buckets, with exceptions.

Name one bucket where an SSE-C write would still succeed, and who would notice.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

At stage 04, Codefinger re-wrote each target object with SSE-C, using an AES-256 key it generated and held. The keys it used carried s3:GetObject and s3:PutObject, which were enough to read existing objects and overwrite them with encrypted copies.

Since then, AWS has disabled SSE-C by default on new general purpose buckets, and on existing buckets in accounts that held no SSE-C encrypted objects. Accounts that already had SSE-C objects kept their configuration, and AWS's FAQ lists two Middle East Regions as exceptions for new buckets.

Ask each person to name one bucket, and who or what would notice an SSE-C write to it, before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says.

== What the page says about SSE-C writes
layout: answer
- Check which case your accounts are in under AWS's new default.
- Where SSE-C is unneeded, deny s3:PutObject with its header, by bucket policy or SCP.
- Turn on S3 data events; alert on PutObject and CopyObject events with that header.
notes:
The page gives prevention and detection.

First, prevention. AWS's default change blocks SSE-C writes on new general purpose buckets and on existing buckets in accounts that held no SSE-C objects; in accounts that already had SSE-C objects, AWS changed no bucket's configuration, so check which case yours is. Where you do not need SSE-C, a bucket policy or SCP that denies s3:PutObject on requests carrying the x-amz-server-side-encryption-customer-algorithm header means an attacker cannot encrypt your objects with a key you do not control.

Second, detection. Turn on S3 data events, which are off by default and cost extra, then monitor for PutObject and CopyObject events carrying that header, and route them to your SIEM.

Bring the group's examples back to this. For each bucket the group named, ask whether it needs SSE-C at all; the page's first defense is for the ones that do not.

== What would you add for stage 03?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to stage 03. The sources do not say how Codefinger found the buckets and objects it encrypted.

What would you add, and which detail on the page would it use?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page offers defenses tied to stages 01, 02, 04 and 05. It ties none to stage 03, where the cited sources do not say how Codefinger found the buckets and objects it encrypted. The page names the calls listing would take if it was used, ListBuckets and ListObjectsV2, calls them ordinary reads that generate no exploitation signal, and says no source reports that Codefinger made them.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stage 03
layout: answer
The page ties no defense to stage 03, and this deck adds none. Any control you propose is your own. The page names only the calls listing would take if used, ListBuckets and ListObjectsV2.
notes:
Be clear that the page ties no defense to stage 03, and this deck adds none of its own.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against what the page says for stage 03. No cited source describes how Codefinger found the buckets and objects. If listing was used, the calls would be ListBuckets, which needs s3:ListAllMyBuckets, and ListObjectsV2, which needs s3:ListBucket: ordinary reads that generate no exploitation signal. No source reports that Codefinger made them, so do not build a proposal on the claim that it did.

Some proposals will lean on the page's own defenses. Alerting on unfamiliar source IPs on existing keys is one. The matrix ties it to stage 02's detection gap, not to stage 03, so stretching it to stage 03 is the group's own argument. Keeping backups in a separate account the leaked key cannot reach is another. Stage 03 lists backups among typical targets, but the page does not tie that defense to stage 03, so that argument is the group's own too.

